Good day, all,
	As I mentioned a month or so back, Jeff Dike has had some success 
with UML on the AMD64 architecture (Opteron and Athlon64).  As part of 
shaking out the bugs on that code, we have a new UML coop going up in the 
near future - codenamed "Ford".
	Each of the participants will split the cost of the core hardware 
purchase.  We're lucky enough to have two ISP's offering to host the box 
at no charge, so all we need to cover is the initial purchase and any 
upgrades.
	In exchange for that, each person gets a 64 bit virtual machine 
for their own use.  Because this is development code, there are no 
promises of perfect stability, so don't expect to run your storefront on 
it.

	After some discussion on the Ford mailing list, we've come down to 
these specs for the new box:

Processors: Opteron 240x2 OEM - $384
Memory: Gigaram DDR400x2 (2GB total) Lifetime warranty - $630
HD: Hitachi SATA 250Gbx2 - $384
Motherboard: Tyan K8S Pro - $515
        2xSocket954
        4xSATA with RAID 0/1/0+1
        8xDIMM (max 16GB)
        Integrated ATI RAGE XL 8Mb
        Integrated SCSI Ultra320
        Integrated Dual GB ethernet, plus one additional 10/100 ethernet
DVD: $15
Case: Enermax 2U ICS217B EATX - $274
        400W power supply
        7x3.5"
        1x5.25"
Fans (low profile cpu, case, etc.): $100

Total:  $2302

Knowing that you're helping to debug UML and advance Linux: Priceless


	If you have some interest in taking part, please start at
http://www.stearns.org/ford/ford-project.html .  Please sign up for the
mailing list ( http://www.whoopis.com/mailman/listinfo/ford/ ); all
discussion happens there.  Send in a note saying that you're interested
and what you'd be willing to commit to for the purchase.  We plan to order
the hardware in the next week or two, but before we can do that we need to
know the final number of participants so we can get an appropriate amount
of hardware.  If more people come on we can get more ram, disk space,
and/or CPU.
	Cheers,
	- Bill

